  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp upward spike (e.g., Example 1 & 2), the model fails to capture immediate momentum and underestimates the first few days of the forecast horizon by 3-5%.",
    "The model exhibits significant mean reversion bias in volatile regimes; when true outcomes show high volatility followed by a drop, the model incorrectly predicts a smooth, linear continuation upward.",
    "Sentiment history (0.20-0.50) appears to trigger delayed reaction effects rather than immediate price alignment, causing the model to lag behind sentiment-driven spikes for 3-5 days.",
    "In bearish or sideways regimes (Example 5), the model's linear projection is consistently lower than actual outcomes during the first half of the horizon, suggesting an underestimation of short-term volatility recovery.",
    "The model systematically ignores the 'mean reversion' component of EU ETS prices, assuming a constant drift rate that fails when historical variance increases."
  ]
